      'Monosaccharides are polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones. In solution a hexose such as glucose exists almost entirely as a six-membered ring, formed when the hydroxyl group on C-5 adds to the carbonyl carbon, C-1, and converts the aldehyde into a cyclic hemiacetal. The former carbonyl carbon, now bonded to two oxygen atoms, is the anomeric carbon, and the hydroxyl group attached to it can lie on either face of the ring, giving the α and β anomers. Because the ring opens and closes continuously, the two anomers and a small amount of the open-chain form coexist at equilibrium.\n\nWhen the anomeric hydroxyl of one sugar condenses with a hydroxyl group of a second molecule, the product is a glycosidic bond. The anomeric carbon involved is now an acetal and is locked: it can no longer open to the free carbonyl. A disaccharide in which only one anomeric carbon takes part in the bond retains a hemiacetal at its other end; a disaccharide in which both anomeric carbons form the bond has none.\n\nBenedict’s reagent, an alkaline solution of Cu²⁺ complexed with citrate, is reduced by sugars that can present a free aldehyde group, with formation of a red-brown precipitate of Cu₂O; sugars that respond are called reducing sugars. The acetal linkage of a glycoside is stable in base but is cleaved by hot dilute acid, which hydrolyzes glycosidic bonds without regard to their configuration. Glycosidase enzymes, in contrast, hydrolyze only bonds of one specific configuration: maltase (an α-glucosidase) cleaves the α(1→4) bond between two glucose units, lactase (a β-galactosidase) cleaves the β(1→4) bond that joins galactose to glucose, and sucrase cleaves the bond in sucrose, which joins the anomeric carbon of glucose to the anomeric carbon of fructose.\n\nFive carbohydrate samples, labeled V through Z, were each supplied as a pure compound at a concentration of 10.0 mM. Students were told that each sample was either a single hexose or a disaccharide made of two hexose units, and that no two samples were the same compound. Five 1.00-mL portions of each sample were prepared. One portion was tested directly with Benedict’s reagent. A second was heated with 1 M HCl for 30 min, neutralized, and then tested. Each of the remaining three was incubated with one of the three glycosidases at 37 °C and pH 7 for 1 h and then tested. In every test the Cu₂O precipitate was collected, dried, and weighed, and its mass was expressed as a reducing value relative to the mass produced by a 1.00-mL portion of 10.0 mM glucose, which was assigned the value 1.00. Under these conditions a given mass of precipitate was found to be proportional to the number of moles of reducing sugar present. The results are given in Table 1.\n\nIn a separate experiment, the open-chain form of Sample W was shown to contain a ketone group rather than an aldehyde group.',
