      'Most drugs that act on the nervous system are amines. An amine is a derivative of ammonia in which one, two, or three hydrogens are replaced by carbon groups; it is classified as primary, secondary, or tertiary according to the number of carbon atoms bonded to the nitrogen, not according to the substitution of those carbons. When a fourth carbon group is attached, the nitrogen carries a permanent positive charge and the compound is a quaternary ammonium salt. The lone pair on the nitrogen of a primary, secondary, or tertiary amine makes it a base: it is protonated by acid to give an ammonium ion. Basicity is conventionally expressed as the $\\text{p}K_a$ of the conjugate ammonium ion, so that a higher $\\text{p}K_a$ corresponds to a stronger base. For a base B and its conjugate acid BH⁺,\n\n$\\text{pH} = \\text{p}K_a + \\log\\frac{[\\text{B}]}{[\\text{BH}^+]}$\n\nThe ammonium ions of simple alkylamines have $\\text{p}K_a$ values near 10, whereas those of aromatic amines, in which the nitrogen is bonded directly to a benzene ring, have $\\text{p}K_a$ values near 4 to 5. Amides are not appreciably basic under physiological conditions.\n\nThe charge state of an amine controls its movement through the body. A lipid bilayer is readily crossed by the small neutral molecule but is essentially impermeable to the charged ammonium ion. When a membrane separates two aqueous compartments of different pH, the neutral form equilibrates across the membrane until its concentration is the same on both sides, but the concentration of the ionized form on each side is set by that side’s pH. The total concentration of drug, neutral plus ionized, therefore differs between the compartments, a phenomenon called ion trapping. For a weak base, the ratio of total concentrations at equilibrium is\n\n$\\frac{C_B}{C_A} = \\frac{1 + 10^{(\\text{p}K_a - \\text{pH}_B)}}{1 + 10^{(\\text{p}K_a - \\text{pH}_A)}}$\n\nwhere $C_A$ and $C_B$ are the total concentrations in compartments A and B. For a weak acid, the sign of each exponent is reversed.\n\nTo measure ion trapping directly, investigators used a cell with two 10-mL compartments separated by a synthetic lipid membrane. Compartment A was buffered at pH 7.4 to represent plasma, and compartment B at pH 6.4 to represent mildly acidic urine. Each of four test compounds was added to compartment A at an initial concentration of 100 μM, the cell was gently stirred for 24 h at 37 °C, and the total concentration of the compound in each compartment was then measured. Compound 1 is a tertiary alkylamine bearing two N-methyl groups; Compound 2 is a primary aromatic amine; Compound 3 is the N-methyl quaternary ammonium derivative of Compound 1; and Compound 4 is a carboxylic acid. The $\\text{p}K_a$ values and the equilibrium concentration ratios are listed in Table 1. The investigators confirmed that none of the compounds bound measurably to the membrane itself and that the pH of each compartment did not drift during the experiment.',
